WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.080
Hardening is incomplete without visibility and recovery.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.420
System logs, authentication logs, process and service events, endpoint alerts,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.120
configuration changes, backup status,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.340
and update results help teams understand what changed and what happened.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.000
Logs are especially important when troubleshooting a failed change,

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.020
investigating suspicious activity, or confirming that a baseline is actually applied.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.480
Monitoring should produce actionable signals.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.420
Endpoint protection alerts, failed authentication patterns, unexpected service changes,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.400
disabled agents, backup failures, low disk space,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.500
and repeated update failures all deserve attention.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.760
The goal is not to collect every possible event forever.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.020
The goal is to give administrators, SOC analysts,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.800
and system owners enough evidence to detect problems and respond responsibly.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.500
Backups protect against mistakes, hardware failure, ransomware, corrupted updates,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.240
and bad changes.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.980
A backup that has never been restored is only an assumption.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.680
Recovery testing proves whether files, images, configuration, keys,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.180
and documentation are usable when pressure is real.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.200
Backup readiness should match system criticality:

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.060
a production server and an ordinary workstation may need different recovery objectives.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.720
Visibility and recovery make hardening safer to operate.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.660
If a change causes trouble, logs can explain the failure and backups can support rollback.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.280
If endpoint protection detects a problem, responders need enough context to act.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.460
System hardening should improve prevention, detection,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.440
and recovery together rather than focusing only on configuration.
